== By Grace Through Faith

#quote[“By grace you#super[+] have been saved through faith.” — Eph 2:8.]

I #smallcaps[think] it wise to step aside a little, so that I may ask my reader to look with adoration at #emph[the fountainhead] of our salvation, which is the grace of God. “By grace you#super[+] have been saved.” Because God is gracious, sinful men are forgiven, converted, purified, and saved. It is not because of anything in them, or anything that ever can be in them, that they are saved; but because of the boundless love, goodness, pity, compassion, mercy, and grace of God. Stop for a moment, then, at the wellspring. See the pure river of the water of life, as it flows out from the throne of God and of the Lamb!

What a bottomless deep the grace of God is! Who can measure its breadth? Who can fathom its depth? Like all the rest of the divine attributes, it is infinite. God is full of love, for “God is love.” God is full of goodness; the very name “God” is short for “good.” Boundless goodness and love are part of the very essence of the Godhead. It is because “His mercy endures forever” that men are not destroyed; because “His compassions never fail” that sinners are brought to him and forgiven.

Remember this well, or you#super[+] may fall into error by fixing your minds so much on the faith which is the channel of salvation that you forget the grace which is the fountain and source even of faith itself. Faith is the work of God’s grace in us. No man can say that Jesus is the Christ except by the Holy Spirit. “No one can come to Me,” says Jesus, “unless the Father who sent Me draws him.” So faith, which is coming to Christ, is the result of God’s drawing. Grace is the first and last moving cause of salvation; and faith, essential as it is, is only an important part of the machinery which grace uses. We are saved “through faith,” but salvation is “by grace.” Sound out those words as if with the archangel’s trumpet: “By grace you#super[+] have been saved.” What good news for the undeserving!

Faith holds the place of a #emph[channel or conduit]. Grace is the fountain and the stream; faith is the aqueduct along which the flood of mercy flows down to refresh the thirsty sons of men. It is a great pity when the aqueduct is broken. It is a sad sight to see, around Rome, the many noble aqueducts which no longer carry water into the city, because the arches are broken and those marvelous structures are in ruins. The aqueduct must be kept whole to carry the flow; and in the same way faith must be true and sound, reaching right up to God and coming right down to us, so that it may become a useful channel of mercy to our souls.

Even so, I remind you again that faith is only the channel or aqueduct, and not the fountainhead, and we must not look at it so much that we exalt it above the divine source of all blessing, which lies in the grace of God. #emph[Never make a Christ out of your faith], and do not think of it as if it were the independent source of your salvation. Our life is found in “looking to Jesus,” not in looking to our own faith. By faith all things become possible to us; yet the power is not in the faith, but in the God on whom faith relies. Grace is the locomotive, and faith is the chain by which the carriage of the soul is coupled to the great driving power. The righteousness of faith is not the moral excellence of faith, but the righteousness of Jesus Christ, which faith grasps and makes its own. The peace within the soul does not come from looking at our own faith; it comes to us from him who is our peace: faith touches the hem of his garment, and power comes out of him into the soul.

See, then, dear friend, that the weakness of your faith will not destroy you. A trembling hand may receive a golden gift. The Lord’s salvation can come to us even if our faith is only like a grain of mustard seed. The power lies in the grace of God, and not in our faith. Great messages can be sent along slender wires, and the peace-giving witness of the Holy Spirit can reach the heart by means of a faith as thin as a thread, which seems hardly able to bear its own weight. Think more of #smallcaps[him] to whom you look than of the look itself. You must look away even from your own looking, and see nothing but Jesus, and the grace of God revealed in him.
